  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Constructing Server.ts**\n\nI\'m currently focused on designing `/server.ts` for a full-stack Express server. The key considerations revolve around development-style serving of the Vite app. I\'m evaluating different strategies, like proxying requests and running Vite middleware. Since we are running on port 5173, perhaps the middleware would be more appropriate.\n\n\n**Clarifying Deployment Strategy**\n\nI\'m now contemplating how to integrate the build and start processes for production deployment. The core idea is to serve static assets from `/dist` and manage API endpoints through the Express server. I\'m exploring the structure of standard full-stack projects using Express and Vite. Additionally, the need to adapt to the development environment on port 3000 adds complexity, specifically how to combine Vite and Express. I\'m wondering if a dual setup is overkill.\n\n\n**Implementing Middleware in Dev**\n\nI\'ve decided to integrate Vite as middleware in development. This approach should simplify things, especially given that Vite\'s development server handles hot module replacement. I\'m focusing on the implementation using the provided example code. I\'ve also incorporated a basic health endpoint for API testing.\n\n\n**Refining Server Structure**\n\nI\'m thrilled with the current design! This structure perfectly manages both development (Vite middleware) and production (static assets), all running smoothly on port 3000. My immediate focus is on ensuring all Node modules, particularly `path`, are imported accurately. I\'m writing this exact `server.ts` structure, confident it\'s a robust standard for Vite+Express apps.\n\nNow I am ready to implement the AI Speech-to-Text Transcription API, on the endpoint `/api/transcribe`, and am ready to review the options for file uploads.\n\n\n**Defining Transcription Process**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the transcription API endpoint, `/api/transcribe`. I\'m exploring file upload options. Since Gemini 3.5-flash now accepts video/audio with inline data (up to ~20MB!), sending base64 or audio bytes seems feasible for typical clips. I\'m exploring how to send video data directly to the backend. Now, my focus is refining the Gemini API input to include the correct data, and to request JSON subtitles!\n\n\n**Designing Subtitle Schema**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough! I found how to receive structured JSON responses from Gemini! I can design a subtitle schema now for time-stamped transcription segments. I\'m building a robust `subtitleSchema` for the `/api/transcribe` endpoint now. It\'ll define `id`, `startTime`, `endTime`, and `text` for each subtitle block.\n\n\n**Crafting Transcription Instructions**\n\nI\'ve crafted a system instruction for Gemini that should work. The prompt tells the AI to create accurate subtitles. I\'ve covered handling multiple speakers (labeling them as \'Speaker 1\', \'Speaker 2\', etc.) and ensured logical subtitle block splitting (3-7 words, 1.5-3 seconds). I\'ve also incorporated punctuation, capitalization, and even filler word removal. I\'m excited to see how this works!\n\n\n**Validating Real Transcription**\n\nI\'m now focusing on verifying the API\'s transcription output, ensuring the JSON subtitle schema is correctly formatted, with matching timestamps. My excitement is growing as the implementation for transcription seems solid!\n\nI\'ve also realized that, in the event of an API failure or to provide a sample, a fallback mechanism is crucial. I plan to implement a simulated, high-fidelity transcription generator. This offers a great user experience: instant captions for sample videos, plus a real AI transcriber!\n\n\n**Expanding AI Features**\n\nI\'m now outlining key AI-driven enhancements: auto-correction (`/api/suggest-corrections`), filler word removal (toggle in transcription config and editing UI), smart punctuation (toggle under AI settings), and speaker detection (included in standard transcript with customizable labels).\n\n\n**Planning Editing Features**\n\nI\'m now diving into the user editing features! I\'m planning out the UI elements and interactions for manual subtitle adjustments. I need to design for text editing, time adjustments, block splitting/merging, and adding/removing subtitle segments. I\'m focusing on ease of use and intuitiveness for the core functionalities.\n\n\n\n**Envisioning UI Interactions**\n\nI\'m now consumed with designing the user interface. I\'m focusing on animated caption styles, like pop, karaoke, and zoom effects, and thinking of the potential for drag-and-drop customization and social media style layouts. I need to balance the single-page constraint with an impressive and interactive visual experience.\n\n\n**Designing UI Theme**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a sleek, dark theme for the UI, drawing inspiration from professional video editing software. I\'m leaning toward a matte slate and charcoal color scheme to reflect a workspace feel. The single-screen design emphasizes a professional video workbench layout.\n\n\n**Conceptualizing UI Layout**\n\nI\'m now putting together the UI\'s right-side panel: upload settings, subtitles editor, and styling/animation presets, envisioning animated captions and draggable timeline adjustments. I\'m focusing on user-friendly interactions and a single-screen, multi-section workflow with styling presets for pop, karaoke, and zoom effects. I\'m prioritizing an intuitive design for text, time, and style controls.\n\n\n**Defining Export Options**\n\nI\'m now focusing on export options. While client-side \"burned\" video export poses challenges, I\'m prioritizing SRT, VTT, TXT and Project JSON export. I\'ll need to explore the feasibility of the `MediaRecorder` API or even explain a simulated rendering approach.\nI\'m now starting to implement the `/server.ts` Express server file. Let\'s make sure it imports everything correctly.\nI need to research libraries for parsing base64 file uploads!\n\n\n**Implementing Server.ts**\n\nI\'m now starting server-side development, specifically `/server.ts`. I\'ve realized `express.json({ limit: \'100mb\' })` should handle base64 uploads. Plus, the server can run with `tsx` directly, no extra build steps, which is great! I need to ensure the correct imports for Gemini and `dotenv`. I\'m starting to build a robust file for the transcription API endpoint, keeping in mind how to integrate Google GenAI and handle video uploads. The spoken language can also be configured.\n\n\n**Refining Gemini Transcription**\n\nI\'m now implementing the Gemini transcription endpoint `/api/transcribe`, and have focused on sending base64 audio/video with inline data. I\'m now crafting the server-side logic for Gemini, specifying the language and configuration like filler word removal and smart punctuation. I\'m working with the Gemini API to receive JSON-formatted subtitles with timestamps, and the user-specified configuration. I\'m also ensuring the correct mimeType is sent!\n\n\n**Refining API Transcription**\n\nI\'m now integrating AI transcription functionality! I am actively implementing the `/api/transcribe` endpoint, with the system instructions from before. I\'m focusing on the Gemini API calls. I\'m verifying the input/output with various audio and video samples, with the configured settings. I\'m refining how to handle speaker detection. The implementation now handles the JSON schema for time-stamped subtitles.\n\n\n\n**Optimizing Data Transmission**\n\nI\'m now laser-focused on optimizing video uploads for transcription! I\'ve realized we can leverage the Web Audio API in the browser to extract audio from video files and encode it as lightweight WAV files. This dramatically reduces the upload size, improving performance. The user experience is enhanced with audio-only transmission instead of bulky video uploads! I\'m planning to implement client-side audio extraction and encoding now.\n\n\n**Optimizing Client-Side Audio**\n\nI\'m implementing client-side audio extraction using the Web Audio API to convert video to WAV. This dramatically boosts upload speed and reliability! I\'ve designed a fallback for video uploads or chunked video uploads, if audio extraction fails. This will be integrated into the transcription API to streamline the process. The server will now receive lightweight audio instead of bulky video.\n\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create `/server.ts`, a full-stack Express server that serves our React frontend, configures the Gemini API client correctly, and integrates a high-quality, real Speak-to-Text transcription endpoint `/api/transcribe` using Gemini 3.5-flash with a structured JSON schema. I will also add API endpoints for AI smart features (Filler Word Cleanup, AI Timeline Punctuation & Corrections)."
