  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Listing Visual Properties**\n\nI\'m now outlining the visual properties and components needed for `/src/App.tsx`. The primary focus is on a video upload section with both drag-and-drop and sample selection capabilities. I\'m taking the features into account, such as the requirement of clean drag-and-drop, sample options, and progress indication. I\'m aiming to define exactly what is requested, before further design.\n\n\n**Defining Upload Support**\n\nI\'m solidifying the details for video uploading. I\'m expanding on the requirements for broad file format support. In addition, I\'m now incorporating pre-loaded sample videos. The plan is to offer users an immediate trial experience. I am currently considering three mock projects: \"Podcast Interview,\" \"Tech Tutorial Vlog,\" and \"Travel Reel,\" each paired with a sample URL and subtitle timeline for a complete studio experience.\n\n\n**Designing Interactive Media**\n\nI\'m now diving into interactive video elements! I\'m envisioning a standard HTML5 video player enhanced with custom aesthetic controls and a dynamic caption canvas. This is super exciting, as I am envisioning a video container that has relative positioning that hosts an overlay div for text captions. I\'m focusing on features such as font controls, background styling (transparent to glow), and interactive drag-and-drop for caption placement.\n\n\n**Implementing Dynamic Subtitles**\n\nI\'m now implementing dynamic subtitles, which is cool! I\'m creating active captions that are styled based on the user\'s selected preset. I\'m focusing on word-by-word highlights and dynamic styles. The underlying mechanism involves determining the active subtitle using the current video time. Then, I\'ll split the subtitle text into individual words for synchronization. Finally, I\'ll map each word to its specific sub-interval within the active subtitle\'s duration.\n\n\n**Exploring Caption Animation**\n\nI\'m now exploring animation presets for captions! I\'m planning to implement several visual effects. These range from subtle changes like dimming inactive words to dramatic ones such as the \"TikTok\" style, which is very exciting. I am considering presets like \"Pop,\" \"Bounce,\" and \"Zoom.\" In addition, I will be incorporating \"Fade,\" and \"Speaker Highlight Mode\" for enhanced visual presentation and engagement.\n\n\n**Constructing Editor Features**\n\nI\'m now building out the features of the subtitle editor! I\'m planning a vertical panel for each subtitle block that offers extensive controls. Each card will be editable to include text, the speaker, and start/end times. I\'ve designed these to enable on-the-fly corrections. Users can seek the video player to the block\'s `startTime` upon selection. I\'ve also implemented split, merge, and delete options, along with an \"add\" feature for new blocks.\n\n\n**Integrating Timeline Interactivity**\n\nI\'m now integrating enhanced timeline interactivity! Clicking the timeline seeks the video. Subtitle blocks are drawn as draggable pill-bars, and dragging adjusts `startTime` and `endTime` in real-time, which is an elite feature! I am so excited about how this is coming together. The functionality will provide a premium user experience!\n\n**Implementing AI Panel**\n\nI\'m implementing an AI Smart Panel for automated improvements! I\'m planning auto-correction features that display suggested subtitle improvements from `/api/suggest-corrections`, such as the example correction: \"\'Gimini\' to \'Gemini\'\". I\'m excited by this very detailed AI integration! It will have a button that applies each suggestion. I\'m also planning filler word detection and smart punctuation with an automated cleanup option.\n\n**Defining Export Options**\n\nI\'m now defining export options! I\'m implementing an \"Export SRT\" feature, which will provide users with a versatile export feature. I\'m now exploring other export formats and options to enhance the feature set!\n\n\n**Expanding Export Functionality**\n\nI\'m now expanding the export functionality to include various formats for maximum user utility! I\'m planning to implement SRT, VTT, TXT transcript, and project file exports. I\'m also planning the \"Burned-In Subtitle Render,\" which guides users on achieving baked-in subtitles using either canvas capture or HTML MediaRecorder, or gives step-by-step guidance on embedding exported files. I\'m also now planning audio extraction functionality.\n\n\n\n**Extracting Audio Details**\n\nI\'m now extracting audio from the video file! I\'ve decided to convert the audio into a lightweight WAV base64 data URL for efficiency. I\'m using the Web Audio API with `OfflineAudioContext` for high performance. I am now working on the implementation in `/src/App.tsx`, and I\'ve sketched a helper function to facilitate this.\n\n\n**Refining Audio Encoding**\n\nI\'m now refining the audio encoding process for increased efficiency and reliability! I\'m now encoding the rendered buffer as WAV format with a mono channel, optimizing for faster processing. I\'m focusing on ensuring data integrity during this conversion. I am making use of `encodeWAV` to facilitate this conversion! I\'m creating a `Blob` from `wavBytes`, with `FileReader` for the `dataURL`.\n\n\n**Optimizing WAV Encoding**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the WAV encoding implementation. I\'ve broken down the intricacies of the WAV file format, focusing on writing the file headers and handling the data chunks. I am especially focusing on the sample rate, channel count, byte rate, and block alignment parameters, and how to write the 16-bit PCM data. I am using `writeString` to make sure all is done right!\n\n\n\n**Perfecting Audio Extraction**\n\nI\'m now achieving audio extraction perfection! I\'m creating a masterpiece audio extractor in pure TypeScript that solves the backend upload issue. My design decodes any uploaded video or audio, grabs the audio channel, downsamples to 16000Hz WAV (matching Gemini), encodes it as a standard WAV, and converts to a base64 dataURL for efficient transport to Gemini, making file sizes and processing instant! I am also implementing a fallback for browser limitations.\n\n\n**Defining Sample Clips**\n\nI\'m now designing the predefined sample clips in `/src/App.tsx`. I\'ve settled on three Google GTV videos as reliable, permanent, and fast CDN URLs for a podcast, tech vlog, and travel reel. They will load and play perfectly inside the video element.\n\n\n**Populating Sample Presets**\n\nI\'m now populating the state with styled subtitles based on selected presets! I\'ve defined the content of three preloaded sample projects. Sample 1, a multi-speaker podcast, includes captions from Speaker 1 and 2 with precise timestamps. I have designed Sample 2 as a solo tech review, and Sample 3 as a travel vlog, each with subtitle timelines. I\'m excited by how this creates a great starting experience!\n\n\n**Designing UI Controls**\n\nI\'m now detailing the user interface controls within a polished design. I\'m focusing on the video frame, a custom video player deck (Play/Pause, seek slider, time indicator, speed selector, subtitle visibility toggle, and a fullscreen simulator to substitute for the browser\'s), and a floating caption container overlay with precise styling. The focus here is on ensuring the controls are intuitive and aesthetically pleasing.\n\n\n**Implementing Caption Interactions**\n\nI\'m now implementing interactive captions using React pointer listeners for intuitive drag-and-drop functionality within the video container. In parallel, I\'m designing a timeline scroller track that visualizes subtitle segments as colored bars with corresponding start and end times, complete with double-click seeking and drag-able timing adjustments. I\'m focusing on user-friendly interactions.\n\n**Designing The Right Panel**\n\nI\'m now meticulously designing the Right Panel! I\'m planning a tabbed interface for streamlined subtitle management, style configuration, and AI workspace integration. I\'m prioritizing a dense and professional aesthetic, which will have a single screen with tabs. I am incorporating an AI Transcription tab with a custom file selector, transcription parameters (language, filler word removal, smart punctuation, speaker identification), and a prominent \"Generate Subtitles\" button to enhance the workflow.\n\n\n**Defining Caption Presets**\n\nI\'m now implementing aesthetic theme presets! I\'m creating a \"Captions Style & Animated Presets\" panel with clickable preview badges, such as \"TikTok Shorts,\" \"Classic Cinema,\" \"Future Cyberpunk,\" and \"Karaoke Host,\" each with their unique visual styles. I\'m focusing on \"TikTok Shorts\" with a Zoom/Bounce animation. There\'s also typography settings for font family, size, color and opacity. I\'m excited to provide a range of aesthetic options!\n\n\n**Refining Aesthetic Controls**\n\nI am now implementing advanced caption style controls! I\'m adding bold/regular and italic toggles, alongside letter spacing adjustments (tighter, normal, wide). I\'m also including a background color picker and opacity slider, plus text shadow options with a size slider and color picker. I am also implementing a text outline with a size and color picker, and a glow intensity slider with a color picker!\n\n\n**Defining AI Integration**\n\nI\'m now detailing AI integration within the right panel. I\'m focusing on an auto-correction engine that scans subtitles and displays suggestions from `/api/suggest-corrections`, with \"Apply\" buttons. I\'m also planning a filler word cleaner and smart punctuation features, triggered by dedicated buttons to enhance subtitle quality. I am now also going to verify dependencies!\n\n\n**Structuring App Component**\n\nI\'m now structuring the imports and state management within `/src/App.tsx` for optimal organization. I\'ve defined states for subtitles, `currentTime`, `isPlaying`, `videoUrl`, and `isUploading`, including individual configurations. Also including visual styles, such as font family, font size, and text color, for a clean and adaptable setup! I am using framer-motion and beautiful icons.\n\n\n"
