  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Playback State**\n\nI\'ve been examining some code related to video playback. Specifically, I\'m focusing on how the `isPlaying` state variable is managed. It appears `setIsPlaying(true)` is set on the `play` event and, presumably, `setIsPlaying(false)` on `pause` events. This approach seems straightforward for tracking the video\'s current state.\n\n\n**Considering Error Handling**\n\nI\'ve realized a significant edge case regarding video playback: what if the video source fails to load? Currently, I\'m thinking about how to handle scenarios where video URLs fail, the browser lacks the necessary codecs, or network issues arise. My current thought is to provide helpful UI alerts within the video container, suggesting alternative sources or upload options, even offering a simulated player for testing.\n\n\n**Implementing Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on handling video playback errors. I\'ve realized that listening to the `error` event is key, which lets me update a `videoError` state. When this state is active, I\'ll display a notification card with helpful alternatives. More excitingly, I\'m now planning a \"Synthetic Media Player Simulator\" mode! It will feature a dynamic SVG/CSS visualizer for simulating video playback and preserving full app functionality, even with source issues. This solves many potential platform limitations. I am now confident this is exactly what the user wants.\n\n\n**Developing Fallback Strategies**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on handling a common playback interruption error: \"The element has no supported sources.\" My focus is creating a robust fallback, as a high-quality video tool should have. The goal is to provide a fallback URL using a free-codec WebM stream to solve compatibility issues that may arise with proprietary codecs like H.264. I\'m now seeking WebM test streams like those from Wikimedia.\n\n\n**Defining Fallback URLs**\n\nI\'m now integrating Wikimedia\'s streams, ensuring high availability and codec compatibility across various environments. I\'m focusing on open-source WebM formats (VP8/VP9/Ogg/Theora) to ensure flawless headless and basic browser playback. I\'m planning to define backup URLs for each sample project, or even give the user a way to toggle a fallback WebM stream! I\'m comparing the Google Cloud Storage MP4s with this option for resilience. I\'ve realized, on top of this, that I must add a state `virtualVideoMode`.\n\n\n**Implementing Error Handling**\n\nI\'ve successfully implemented error handling with a `videoError` handler that activates on the `<video>` element\'s `error` event, flawlessly handling volume and captions. Now, with either a `videoError` or `virtualVideoMode` active, I\'ve designed a captivating visualizer for the video container. This visualizer, perfectly themed with the slate/blue Bento Grid aesthetic, features animated elements like abstract waveforms, retro tape reels, or pulse visualizations! It also includes a clear warning and a toggle for switching between \"Synthetic Player Simulator\" and \"HTML5 Video Player Codecs\". I\'m now examining the `src/App.tsx` file, specifically the hooks and the `useEffect` around line 160, to integrate this feature seamlessly.\n\n\n**Integrating Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now integrating error handling directly into the video element. I\'m adding `handleVideoError` to manage `videoError` and `fallbackMode` states, along with `handleLoadedMetadata`, `handlePlay`, and `handlePause`. My focus is on the `onError` event to pinpoint source issues, and I\'ll clear `videoError` in `handleSelectSample`. The goal is a seamless user experience, with elegant UI cues.\n\n\n**Refining Error Details**\n\nI\'m now refining the video error details. I\'m focusing on the switch statement to pinpoint the error causes and display informative messages. I\'m especially pleased with automatically enabling the simulation preview mode when the error happens; this is really a proactive measure for enhanced UX. I\'m considering simulating play/pause actions in `virtualVideoMode`.\n\n\n**Adapting Playback Logic**\n\nI\'m now modifying the `handlePlayPause` function to manage playback in the `virtualVideoMode`. It looks like I must control a custom timer, since I\'m not directly using the HTML video element. If `virtualVideoMode` is true, I\'ll toggle `isPlaying`, otherwise I\'ll handle the video element. I\'ll need to explore simulating play/pause actions, including `currentTime` advancement.\n\n\n**Simulating Playback Time**\n\nI\'m now implementing the simulated playback ticking, to control `currentTime` when `isPlaying` is true and `virtualVideoMode` is active. I am implementing this inside a `useEffect`! It utilizes `requestAnimationFrame` for smooth advancement based on a custom `playbackSpeed`. I am also handling the end of the video by resetting `currentTime` to zero and setting `isPlaying` to false. This simulation feels incredibly smart and clean.\n\n\n**Refining Simulation Interaction**\n\nI\'m now integrating timeline seeking while in virtual mode. If `virtualVideoMode` is active, setting `video.currentTime` might throw or be a no-op, which is totally safe. I have refactored `handleSeek`, and now setting the `currentTime` will be handled on a separate track only if `!virtualVideoMode`. Now all seeking will seamlessly synchronize between the simulated playback and the app!\n\n\n**Integrating Fallback URLs**\n\nI\'m now integrating the WebM fallback URLs I\'ve identified, and thinking about where to store the URLs in the code. I\'m prioritizing compatibility and availability. I\'m also planning to toggle the `virtualVideoMode` with a button to toggle between the HTML5 video playback and the simulated mode! I\'m now thinking about how to integrate them both without being too verbose. I\'m focusing on defining and switching between HTML5 and virtual streams. I also need to provide this in each sample project.\n\nI am feeling confident.\n\n\n**Implementing Fallback Logic**\n\nI\'m now integrating the WebM fallback strategy, focusing on the `SAMPLE_PROJECTS` definition. My plan is to add a `webmUrl` field to each project, enabling seamless fallback to a compatible WebM source if the primary MP4 fails. This approach allows users to toggle between the original and a fallback stream. I\'m aiming for automatic switching if an error occurs.\n\n\n**Refining Sample Project URLs**\n\nI\'m now integrating the WebM fallback URLs. I\'ve successfully added `webmUrl` fields to each sample project within `SAMPLE_PROJECTS`. I\'m now verifying there is no other reference to `videoUrl` or `webmUrl`. I\'m verifying the current fallback URLs are correct. I\'m prioritizing streamlined playback for any edge cases.\n\n\n**Implementing Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `handleVideoError` function for the fallback mechanism. When an error occurs, it checks if a WebM fallback is available and hasn\'t been tried yet. If so, it switches the `videoUrl` to the WebM source; otherwise, it activates the virtual video mode. This approach ensures graceful degradation and informative feedback for the user. I\'m excited with how easy it is to implement!\n\n\n\n"
